A||||8||Coop88||Is it lowered?||||11||
Z||000000||Coop88||02-01-2001||12:54 AM||||Hi. I was just wondering if any of you could tell me how much clearance you have between your front tire and the top of the fender. Please tell me if you lowered it or not. I was looking at my car and I noticed that the front has alot less clearance compared to the rear. I'm wondering if the previous owner lowered it. Any other way to check? <P>I can't beleive I just noticed this.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><P>Almost forgot, lemme know if you've got stock wheels+tires or not. Thanks!<p>[This message has been edited by Coop88 (edited 02-01-2001).]||||reg||11
Z||000001||mrfiero||02-01-2001||01:12 AM||||On my stock '88 GT (16K miles) I have 3-4 inches of clearance (way too much, but I'm keeping it stock).  <P>I lowered another '88 GT and there might be 1/2-1" clearance.....it's pretty low!   These are the Fiero Store lowered springs.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Eric||02-01-2001||01:22 AM||||The 88s had more of a gap in the front.  Kinda looks like it's being jacked up from the front.  <P>How do you like the Fiero Store springs, mrfiero?  I've heard mixed reviews on them.  Is the price somewhat inline?||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfiero||02-01-2001||01:33 AM||||I don't have any problem with them.  I would not suggest using them with poly bushings up front......too stiff.  Otherwise they are fine.<P>I think the price is fair ($250/set).   ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||02-01-2001||09:24 AM||||My car is lowered with Eibach Pro springs. I have about 1 3/4" of clearence. This is also using non-stock tires. (225/50R15's)<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||elfiero||02-01-2001||11:36 AM||||Mine is slammed pretty hard-I can just fit my<BR>fore finger between the tire and the fender.<BR>I have coilovers on the rear so I can set the<BR>ride height.In the front,I used stock springs<BR>with two coils cut off.This set up works good<BR>'cept when I get a porker in the right seat!<BR>then it rubs just a little.Look at this way,<BR>I don't need an extra air dam on the front<BR>end-its plenty low!||||reg||6
Z||000006||william_thorniii||02-01-2001||12:15 PM||||coop88,<P>We can all speculate, but the fact of the matter is that there were many ride height changes from the factory during the Fiero run.  We would first need to know what year/make/model& miles you have before we could begin to answer your question.  For example, my 87 coupe (90Kmiles) has a tight suspension and has approximately 2" in front and 2.5" in back of space between my extremely bald tires and the wheel wells.  My 86 SE rides much higher.  Although I have never measured it, I would speculate that it is around 1" higher.  <P>Hope this helps,<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||3
Z||000007||Coop88||02-01-2001||02:01 PM||||It's a formula with 150k, needs front upper ball joints(waiting on the ups man), and I have about 2.5 to 3 inches of clearance(that's eyeballing it). Is it possible it looks lower because of the ball joints? I thought that was the job of the shocks/springs. Thanks a bunch for the info so far. ||||reg||10
Z||000008||Eric||02-01-2001||04:56 PM||||I've never been an advocate of cutting the springs, but the selection of 88 springs is pretty weak.  I imagine you could get some coilovers for the rear for the same price as rear springs and then just cut a coil off your fronts.  I personally wouldn't be willing to go much more than that without drop spindles.||||reg||1
